In C, you can terminate a program in several ways:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    return 0;   // Normal successful termination
}
```

Or explicitly:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

For an error condition:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
```

Key distinctions:

- `return` from `main` performs normal termination, equivalent to calling `exit()` with that status.
- `exit(status)` flushes standard I/O streams and runs functions registered with `atexit()`.
- `_Exit(status)` from `<stdlib.h>` terminates immediately without flushing streams or running `atexit()` handlers.
- `abort()` from `<stdlib.h>` terminates abnormally, typically producing a core dump or crash report.

Example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    if (something_went_wrong) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Fatal error\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Conventionally, `0` / `EXIT_SUCCESS` means success; nonzero / `EXIT_FAILURE` signals failure to the calling environment.
